    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the suite is green. A registration date of 2026-09-03 (after 1 July) now correctly maps to fiscal year **FY27** / membership segment `-M27`.\n\n## What I did\n\nIntroduced a small, cohesive `FiscalYear` helper and routed all date-derived values through it, keeping each edited method trivial:\n\n1. **`FiscalYear.java`** (new) \u2014 one focused class with three simple static methods:\n   - `of(date)` \u2014 the fiscal year a date falls in (starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends).\n   - `label(owner)` \u2014 the `FY<YY>` string from the owner's business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`.\n   - `tenure(owner, today)` \u2014 whole fiscal years elapsed since registration.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` field to the Owner schema (drives the generated DTO getter).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` mapping and switched the `membershipNumber` year segment from `registrationDate.getYear()` to the fiscal year. The mapper method itself stays a one-line-per-property interface (CC 1).\n\n4. **`MembershipLevel.points`** \u2014 one line changed: tenure now scores on `FiscalYear.tenure(...) >= 1` instead of a 365-day window (removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import and refreshed the class javadoc).\n\n5. **`OwnerAuditListener`** \u2014 the audit trail emits the same membership identifier, so its year segment now uses the fiscal year too, keeping the API and audit log consistent.\n\nEvery touched method remained CC-simple with little surrounding class complexity; all new behaviour lives in the standalone `FiscalYear` unit rather than growing any existing method or class.\n\n`registrationDate` is already business-day-adjusted upstream (`Owner.defaultRegistrationDate()` applies `BusinessDays.roll` on `@PrePersist`), so deriving the fiscal year from it satisfies the \"business-day-adjusted\" requirement without further changes.",
